\title{Tangled Hierarchies:\\ A Kripke-Semantic Account of Self-Referential Soundness}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a self-contained semantic treatment of \emph{self-referential soundness} in G\"odel--L\"ob provability logic and prove, at the level of Kripke frames, that the soundness (equivalently, consistency) predicate of a sufficiently expressive proof system cannot reside inside the system it validates without trivializing it. Modelling a proof system as a Kripke frame whose worlds are theories and whose accessibility relation encodes ``provable reachability,'' we identify the two structural conditions --- \textbf{transitivity} and \textbf{converse well-foundedness} --- under which the provability modality validates L\"ob's axiom. Within this setting we prove a \emph{semantic L\"ob theorem}, $\Box(\Box A \to A) \subseteq \Box A$, by converse-well-founded induction; we derive the \emph{semantic second incompleteness theorem}, $\Box\,\Con \subseteq \Box\bot$, from the observation that the reflection instance at the false proposition \emph{is} the consistency statement; and we prove the \emph{tangled hierarchy theorem}: no consistent world validates $\Box\,\Con$, so the soundness predicate is unavoidably external. We complement these with a converse --- local self-soundness forces consistency --- and ground the family of results in a single \emph{Lawvere fixed-point} argument, from which Cantor's theorem and Tarski's undefinability of truth follow as the fixed-point-free instance given by Boolean negation. An explicit infinite frame witnesses non-vacuity of every result.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} provability logic, G\"odel--L\"ob logic, L\"ob's theorem, second incompleteness theorem, Kripke semantics, converse well-foundedness, Lawvere fixed-point theorem, Tarski undefinability, self-reference.

\section{Introduction}

A recurring temptation in the design of reasoning systems --- formal theories, program verifiers, self-modelling agents --- is to let a system certify its own reliability. The certificate typically takes the shape of a \emph{soundness} or \emph{consistency} statement: ``everything I prove is true,'' or ``I never prove a contradiction.'' The central fact of this paper is that such a certificate cannot live inside the system it certifies. If it does, the system is either inconsistent (its certificate is vacuous) or it cannot produce the certificate at all. We call the resulting stratification a \textbf{tangled hierarchy}: the level that would validate a system must sit strictly above it.

Our contribution is to present this phenomenon \emph{semantically}, purely in terms of Kripke frames, in a way that is elementary, self-contained, and non-vacuous. The classical route runs through arithmetization of syntax, G\"odel numbering, and the Hilbert--Bernays--L\"ob derivability conditions. We bypass all of that: we work directly with the modal semantics of the provability operator, isolate the two frame conditions that make provability logic tick, and obtain L\"ob's theorem, the second incompleteness theorem, and the impossibility of internal consistency proofs as short semantic lemmas. Beneath them all sits Lawvere's fixed-point theorem, which we prove and use to recover Cantor and Tarski.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A definition of \textbf{G\"odel--L\"ob frames} as transitive, converse-well-founded Kripke frames, with box, diamond, and consistency modalities as operators on subsets of worlds.
  \item A \textbf{semantic L\"ob theorem} proved by converse-well-founded induction, via an explicit maximal-counterexample argument.
  \item The identity \textbf{reflection-at-$\bot$ equals consistency} and, as an immediate corollary, the \textbf{semantic second incompleteness theorem} and the \textbf{tangled hierarchy theorem}.
  \item A \textbf{converse}: local self-soundness at $\bot$ forces consistency.
  \item The \textbf{Lawvere fixed-point theorem} and its corollaries \textbf{Cantor/Tarski}.
  \item A concrete \textbf{infinite witness frame} on which no live world proves its own consistency.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Preliminaries and notation}

A \emph{property} of worlds is identified with the subset of worlds satisfying it. Logical connectives on properties are the corresponding Boolean set operations; the false proposition $\bot$ is the empty set $\varnothing$ and the true proposition $\top$ is the full set of worlds. We write $w \in A$ for ``world $w$ satisfies $A$,'' and $A \subseteq B$ for ``$A$ entails $B$ everywhere.'' For properties $A,B$, the material implication is $A \to B = \{w \mid w \in A \Rightarrow w \in B\}$.

\section{G\"odel--L\"ob frames and the modalities}

\begin{definition}[G\"odel--L\"ob frame]
A \emph{G\"odel--L\"ob frame} $F$ consists of a set $W$ of \emph{worlds} together with a binary \emph{accessibility relation} $R \subseteq W \times W$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{(Transitivity)} for all $a,b,c$, if $R\,a\,b$ and $R\,b\,c$ then $R\,a\,c$;
  \item \textbf{(Converse well-foundedness)} the relation $a \prec b :\Leftrightarrow R\,b\,a$ is well-founded; equivalently, there is no infinite ascending chain $w_0, w_1, w_2, \dots$ with $R\,w_i\,w_{i+1}$, and every nonempty set of worlds has an $R$-maximal element.
\end{itemize}
We read $R\,w\,v$ as ``from $w$, the theory $v$ is a provably reachable continuation.'' Transitivity is axiom \textbf{4}; converse well-foundedness is exactly the frame condition validating L\"ob's axiom.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Box]
For $A \subseteq W$, the \emph{box} (provability) modality is
\[ \Box A = \{\, w \mid \forall v,\ R\,w\,v \Rightarrow v \in A \,\}. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Diamond]
The \emph{diamond} modality is $\Diamond A = \{\, w \mid \exists v,\ R\,w\,v \wedge v \in A \,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Consistency]
The \emph{consistency} property is $\Con = \Diamond\top = \{\, w \mid \exists v,\ R\,w\,v \,\}$. A world is consistent iff it has an accessible successor; a world with no successor is a \emph{dead end} and vacuously validates $\Box A$ for every $A$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity of box]
If $A \subseteq B$ then $\Box A \subseteq \Box B$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $w \in \Box A$ and $R\,w\,v$ then $v \in A \subseteq B$, so $w \in \Box B$.
\end{proof}

\section{The semantic L\"ob theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Semantic L\"ob]\label{thm:loeb}
In every G\"odel--L\"ob frame, $\Box(\Box A \to A) \subseteq \Box A$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Fix $w \in \Box(\Box A \to A)$ and a successor $v$ with $R\,w\,v$; we show $v \in A$. Suppose not, so $v \notin A$. The set $S = \{\, u \mid R\,w\,u \wedge u \notin A \,\}$ contains $v$, hence is nonempty. By converse well-foundedness $S$ has an $R$-maximal element $u$: $R\,w\,u$, $u \notin A$, and no $t$ with $R\,u\,t$ satisfies $t \notin A$ (such $t$ would satisfy $R\,w\,t$ by transitivity, placing $t \in S$ above $u$). Thus every $R$-successor of $u$ lies in $A$, i.e. $u \in \Box A$. From $R\,w\,u$ and $w \in \Box(\Box A \to A)$ we get $u \in (\Box A \to A)$, so $u \in \Box A \Rightarrow u \in A$; with $u \in \Box A$ this yields $u \in A$, contradicting $u \notin A$. Hence $w \in \Box A$.
\end{proof}

Converse well-foundedness is used exactly once (to extract $u$) and transitivity exactly once (to certify $u$'s successors are $w$'s). Both are necessary: on a reflexive point L\"ob's principle fails.

\section{Consistency, incompleteness, and tangling}

\begin{theorem}[Reflection at $\bot$ is consistency]\label{thm:refl}
$\{\, w \mid w \in \Box\bot \Rightarrow w \in \bot \,\} = \Con.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$w \in \Box\bot$ holds iff every successor of $w$ lies in $\varnothing$, i.e. iff $w$ has no successor; and $w \in \bot$ is impossible. So the implication holds iff $w$ has a successor, i.e. $w \in \Con$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Semantic second incompleteness]\label{thm:godel2}
In every G\"odel--L\"ob frame, $\Box\,\Con \subseteq \Box\bot$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Instantiate Theorem~\ref{thm:loeb} at $A = \bot$: $\Box(\Box\bot \to \bot) \subseteq \Box\bot$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:refl} the antecedent equals $\Con$, so $\Box(\Box\bot \to \bot) = \Box\,\Con$; substitution gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tangled hierarchy theorem]\label{thm:tangle}
If $w \in \Con$ (i.e. $w$ has a successor), then $w \notin \Box\,\Con$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $w \in \Box\,\Con$ then $w \in \Box\bot$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:godel2}, so every successor of $w$ lies in $\varnothing$. But $w$ has a successor $v$, whence $v \in \varnothing$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

The dichotomy is exact: a self-certifying world is dead and inconsistent (its certificate vacuous), while a genuinely consistent world cannot produce the certificate at all.

\begin{theorem}[Soundness forces consistency]\label{thm:sound}
If $w \in \Box\bot \Rightarrow w \in \bot$, then $w \in \Con$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is membership of $w$ in the left-hand side of Theorem~\ref{thm:refl}, which equals $\Con$. Concretely, local self-soundness at $\bot$ gives $w \notin \Box\bot$, i.e. $w$ has a successor.
\end{proof}

\section{The diagonal core: Lawvere, Cantor, Tarski}

\begin{theorem}[Lawvere fixed-point]\label{thm:lawvere}
Let $A,B$ be sets and $f : A \to (A \to B)$ be point-surjective (every $A \to B$ equals $f(a)$ for some $a$). Then every endomap $g : B \to B$ has a fixed point.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $d : A \to B$, $d(a) = g(f(a)(a))$. By point-surjectivity $d = f(c)$ for some $c$. Then $f(c)(c) = d(c) = g(f(c)(c))$, so $b := f(c)(c)$ satisfies $g(b) = b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cantor / Tarski undefinability]\label{thm:cantor}
For no set $A$ is there a point-surjective $f : A \to (A \to \Bool)$; a system cannot carry a surjective self-encoding onto its own two-valued predicates.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Boolean negation $\lnot$ has no fixed point. A point-surjective $f$ would, by Theorem~\ref{thm:lawvere} with $g = \lnot$, yield one --- a contradiction.
\end{proof}

L\"ob's theorem is the converse-well-founded refinement of this picture: it does not merely forbid a fixed point of negation but computes the fixed point of the modalized transformer $X \mapsto \Box X \to A$, with converse well-foundedness making the computation terminate rank by rank.

\section{A concrete infinite frame}

\begin{definition}[Natural-number frame]
Let $W = \mathbb{N}$ with $R\,a\,b :\Leftrightarrow b < a$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
The natural-number frame is a G\"odel--L\"ob frame.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Transitivity: $c < b < a \Rightarrow c < a$. Converse well-foundedness: $<$ is well-founded on $\mathbb{N}$, so there is no infinite strictly descending chain.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
$0 \notin \Con$: world $0$ has no successor and is inconsistent. For every $n > 0$, $n \in \Con$ and $n \notin \Box\,\Con$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
No natural is $< 0$, so $0$ is dead. For $n > 0$, $0 < n$ gives $R\,n\,0$, so $n \in \Con$; Theorem~\ref{thm:tangle} yields $n \notin \Box\,\Con$.
\end{proof}

Here $\Box^k\bot = \{0,1,\dots,k-1\}$ is the set of worlds of rank $< k$, $\Con = \{n \geq 1\}$, and $\Box\,\Con$ collapses into $\Box\bot = \{0\}$ --- an explicit instance of the collapse.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\textbf{Formal theories.} These are the semantic skeleton of G\"odel's and L\"ob's theorems: a consistent, sufficiently expressive theory cannot prove its own consistency and can prove ``$A$ is provable $\to A$'' only for provable $A$.

\textbf{Program verification.} A verifier that internally certified its own soundness would, by Theorem~\ref{thm:godel2}, be unsound. Trust is layered: a small kernel is validated externally, each layer certifying only strictly weaker layers.

\textbf{Self-modelling agents.} An agent certifying ``all my conclusions are correct'' endorses every conclusion; calibrated reliability must be represented externally.

\textbf{Foundations.} Section~6 unifies the liar, Cantor, Russell, Tarski, and G\"odel--L\"ob as instances of one fixed-point law, isolating negation as the source of impossibility.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Uniqueness of modal fixed points]
On every transitive, converse-well-founded frame, each modalized predicate transformer has a unique fixed point, definable from finite Boolean combinations of iterated consistency statements. The rank stratification $\Box^k\varnothing = \{\text{rank} < k\}$ forces constancy on each shell.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Exact tangling threshold]
A proof system can internally express its own soundness for all propositions of quantifier-rank below $k$ but never for rank exactly $k$, coinciding with the converse-well-founded rank of the canonical frame; ``consistency = reflection at $\bot$'' is the base rung.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Polymodal tangling]
For modalities $[0],[1],[2],\dots$ of strictly increasing strength, each proves the consistency of all strictly weaker modalities but never its own, and provable cross-consistencies form a strict linear order isomorphic to the modality index.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Diagonal-free certificates]
A proof system admits a diagonal-free consistency certificate iff its canonical frame has an accessible terminal (dead) world reachable in a bounded number of steps.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

The soundness/consistency predicate of an expressive proof system cannot be internal without collapse. The engine is the semantic L\"ob theorem, proved by one maximal-counterexample induction using transitivity and converse well-foundedness; its instance at $\bot$ is the second incompleteness theorem, and the consistency of a live world then forbids any internal consistency proof. Beneath these lies a single Lawvere fixed-point argument that also yields Cantor and Tarski. Tangled hierarchies are a structural law of any system rich enough to reason about its own reasoning.

\end{document}

